WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:13.840
On May 18, 2021, Ziggo, the largest Dutch cable operator, announced to the world that it would no

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.761
longer control the rights for broadcasting Formula 1 in the Netherlands.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.854
What immediately followed was not much, because who actually spends time looking at Ziggo’s Twitter feed? 

00:00:23.854 --> 00:00:29.040
But after Dutch Formula 1 fans realized that this would mean signing up for the streaming service Viaplay

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:34.480
for the very unacceptable Dutch price of 14 euros per month, fans would begin to start getting angry.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.750
What made this change more notable, however, was that the Dutch weren’t alone.

00:00:40.750 --> 00:00:48.080
Only a year before, Nordic Entertainment Group, the owner of Viaplay, also secured a deal that acquired them continued,

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:54.997
exclusive rights for Formula 1 in every single Nordic country from 2022 to 2024.

00:00:54.997 --> 00:00:59.683
This was followed by an expansion into the Baltics, pivoting off of ice hockey,

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:03.555
as well as acquiring exclusive rights for Formula 1 in Poland.

00:01:03.555 --> 00:01:05.509
Their intentions? Clear.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.634
Europe is not known for its streaming services.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:30.296
To no surprise, American media remains dominant everywhere.

00:01:30.296 --> 00:01:33.608
And as the amount of streaming subscriptions increases in Europe,

00:01:33.608 --> 00:01:38.441
Europeans continue to consume mainly American licensed content.

00:01:38.441 --> 00:01:43.227
A question to ask is if you’re a European company that wants to become a streaming giant 

00:01:43.227 --> 00:01:47.074
that could compete on the European market, how could you do it? 

00:01:47.074 --> 00:01:51.680
You could maybe tried to take a bet on an entirely new medium for mass consumption

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.560
and rely on a base of consumers that are eccentric enough to get DVDs delivered through mail.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:02.240
You could rely on decentralized content loaded with ads submitted by users with no social life

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.352
and an inability to tell their parents they’re doing something meaningful with their lives. 

00:02:05.352 --> 00:02:10.152
You could rely on your globally successful brands and people's nostalgia.

00:02:10.152 --> 00:02:13.318
But you could also, of course, just be Jeff Bezos.

00:02:13.318 --> 00:02:17.922
What makes Nordic Entertainment Group, the leader of the pack when it comes to European streaming companies,

00:02:17.922 --> 00:02:25.021
is that it has a combination of strategies that no other European media company has been able to fully replicate.

00:02:25.021 --> 00:02:29.488
They invested in streaming services when it wasn’t financially feasible to do it;

00:02:29.488 --> 00:02:33.687
they rely, and still do, on a bed of nostalgic Nordic content,

00:02:33.687 --> 00:02:36.284
people across the world love&nbsp;
to act like they consume;

00:02:36.284 --> 00:02:40.876
and probably the most important thing is that they did something Netflix hasn’t:

00:02:40.876 --> 00:02:47.280
"...so Netflix is directly going after your territory, it's opening up an office&nbsp;
in the Nordics later this year in Stockholm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.680
it's saying by the end of the year it'll have&nbsp; nearly 70 original titles coming from the Nordics,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.498
so it is going after your space whether you're&nbsp; from the same background or not.

00:02:55.498 --> 00:03:01.680
How will you&nbsp;&nbsp;compete with someone now with such a deep wallet, such a deep pocket as Netflix when it's making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.990
original content like this?

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:08.160
Anders Jensen (Nent CEO): "Well the original&nbsp;content that you refer, it has taken time to build up,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:16.767
we have 129 original titles on our platform&nbsp;now when we're stepping up and we're moving into producing content in other territories as well.

00:03:16.767 --> 00:03:21.770
What we do have that Netflix doesn't have and I do think that the different sort of backgrounds&nbsp;matter,

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:25.809
is that we have a long history of producing world-class sport productions.

00:03:25.809 --> 00:03:33.420
We've moved&nbsp;that over to streaming and we produce more than 50 000 live hours of sports and that's a very&nbsp;different platform experience. 

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:40.640
That allows us to move into new territories in a different&nbsp;way where Netflix and other globals don't compete."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:47.091
Sports are a form of entertainment that has&nbsp;historically been sticky and remained a domain&nbsp;of linear TV.

00:03:47.091 --> 00:03:51.520
Live sports rights are not cheap after all, and a lot of exclusive sports streaming deals

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:56.238
operate at a loss while mainly being used for advertising the larger streaming service.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.779
Live-streaming sports is difficult to do, and rewatching sports would not be

00:04:00.779 --> 00:04:03.564
as common as doing the same for popular TV shows.

00:04:03.564 --> 00:04:07.626
This is why some companies will package sports into documentary style content

00:04:07.626 --> 00:04:11.608
that can stay relevant – profitable – for longer periods of time.

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.105
It's this investment into sports content, even though it would seem like would only

00:04:16.105 --> 00:04:19.560
produce losses, that separates Nordic Entertainment Group.

00:04:20.118 --> 00:04:22.767
They’ve taken a bet on inelastic demand:

00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:28.636
dedicated Formula 1 watchers will go to where Formula 1 is being shown,  even if it means paying a higher price,

00:04:29.136 --> 00:04:32.160
and Nordic Entertainment Group will&nbsp;
then try to keep them with original content&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.730
that they hope will be more profitable in each&nbsp;respective country: 

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.894
Dutch viewers will now be given their own Viaplay original version of Dragon’s den (alongside others),&nbsp;

00:04:40.894 --> 00:04:45.400
Polish viewers 'Polish Murderesses,' and Estonians to ‘Who Shot Otto Mueller.’  

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:50.660
Max Verstappen also announced, along with his father, a multi-year content partnership with Viaplay

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.717
to produce documentary style content even when he originally rejected working

00:04:54.717 --> 00:04:58.240
with Drive to Survive because of its supposed sensationalism.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.044
The reason for paying an insane amount to get Verstappen is simple:

00:05:03.044 --> 00:05:07.119
With almost a cult like following, by acquiring him to produce exclusive content,

00:05:07.119 --> 00:05:11.303
Nordic Entertainment Group can try to turn sceptical Formula 1 motivated,

00:05:11.303 --> 00:05:14.535
potential Dutch subscribers into enthusiastic ones.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.840
In 2018, EU officials slapped on a directive to try and encourage American streaming companies

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:29.376
to uphold some form of minimum European content.

00:05:29.376 --> 00:05:34.420
However, EU directives are practically always legally obscure because it's encouragement by

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.609
the EU for each member state's own specific legislation

00:05:37.609 --> 00:05:40.314
rather than a concrete requirement for what they have to do.

00:05:40.314 --> 00:05:44.000
And either way, the directive may not even be that necessary. 

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:50.000
As subscriber growth stalls in America, streaming services will then try and compete for international markets

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.280
by also producing original content in other countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.324
For Europhiles, this may seem like a good thing in itself.

00:05:56.324 --> 00:05:59.064
But again, there are nuances.

00:05:59.064 --> 00:06:03.039
where do the borders for what is European content begin and end?

00:06:03.039 --> 00:06:08.335
The French have been advocating for an even larger percentage of, air quotes, “European Content,”

00:06:08.335 --> 00:06:10.276
but people know France.

00:06:10.276 --> 00:06:15.553
There is no other European country probably as culturally anxious about Anglo-Saxon content.

00:06:15.553 --> 00:06:21.920
It’s part of the reason some Europeans see the “European” efforts by the French as merely a Trojan horse:

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:28.480
is the 60% French requirement of European content automatically the same as 60% European content, 

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:31.636
or is it, merely, a means for boosting the French?

00:06:31.636 --> 00:06:38.412
It leads to an interesting question: is content originating from Europe important in itself?

00:06:38.412 --> 00:06:42.270
It’s a question I am not qualified to answer because I know nothing, 

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:45.385
and I ironically have way too much of an American accent. 

00:06:45.385 --> 00:06:51.421
But it’s one you can try to by spending your valuable fleeting, conscious time getting angry on the Internet.

